<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime, which notarization requires.

  The hardened runtime is a set of things a signed process may no longer do
  unless it says so here. Every key below is a thing mvpfy actually does: an
  entitlement that is not needed is one more hole in the app, so none of these
  are here for symmetry or by habit.

  This file is used for the app and inherited by Electron's helper processes
  (entitlementsInherit in package.json), because the renderer and GPU helpers
  need the same JIT permissions the main process does.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    V8 compiles JavaScript to machine code at runtime and executes it. Without
    these two the renderer is killed on launch — this is not a mvpfy choice,
    it is what running a JavaScript engine means.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    mvpfy runs agents and tools as child processes through the user's own login
    shell, so the environment their dotfiles set has to survive into them. That
    is the whole point of going through a login shell: finding the same `claude`,
    `codex`, `docker` and `git` the user finds in their own terminal.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Electron loads its own frameworks and the app's unpacked native modules from
    inside the bundle. Library validation would require every one of them to
    carry this app's team signature, which the prebuilt ones do not.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    The one that is easy to miss, and that breaks a feature rather than the
    whole app: mvpfy opens Terminal.app through AppleScript to run installs and
    long-lived commands where the user can watch and answer them (see
    electron/services/shell.ts and install.ts). Under the hardened runtime,
    sending an Apple event to another application is denied without this, and
    the failure is a silent errAEEventNotPermitted rather than a crash — so the
    Terminal button would simply stop doing anything.

    It also needs NSAppleEventsUsageDescription in Info.plist, set in
    package.json, or macOS refuses before it even asks the user.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
